   $q$Triangle 1 has two angles that measure $48^\circ$ and $71^\circ$. Triangle 2 has two angles that measure $71^\circ$ and $61^\circ$. Which statement is true?$q$, $q$[{"text":"There is not enough information, because side lengths are needed to decide","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-similarity-needs-side-lengths"},{"text":"The triangles are similar, because all three pairs of angles match","is_correct":true},{"text":"The triangles are not similar, because only one of the listed angles matches","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"did-not-find-third-angle"},{"text":"The triangles are congruent, because their angles match","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"thinks-equal-angles-mean-congruent"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The triangles are similar, because all three pairs of angles match$q$, $q$The third angle of Triangle 1 is $180 - 48 - 71 = 61$ degrees, and the third angle of Triangle 2 is $180 - 71 - 61 = 48$ degrees. Both triangles have angles $48^\circ$, $61^\circ$, and $71^\circ$, so they are similar. Matching angles alone do not show the triangles are the same size, so they need not be congruent.$q$, 'draft', null)
